    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d387">

                <pb facs="0394" n="382" sameAs="frus:frus1945v04/pg382"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">874.00/11–1445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-11-15T21:00:00-05:00">November 15,
                            1945—9 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2348. For Ethridge. You will have received a copy of the note which Barnes is
                    instructed to deliver to the Bulgarian authorities in <pb facs="0395" n="383"
                        xml:id="pg383"/> respect to the Bulgarian elections.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d387-f1"/> Unless you consider it desirable to have further
                    conversations with Molotov or Vyshinski in this respect I agree that you should
                    proceed to Rumania as suggested in your tel 3859 Nov 14 leaving negotiations
                    with regard Bulgaria to normal channels.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d387-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d387-f1" n="7" xml:id="d387-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram 373, November 14, 7 p.m., <ref target="#pg376">p. 376</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d387-f2" n="8" xml:id="d387-f2-content">
                <p>Mr. Ethridge left Moscow on November 18.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
